%[text] # Task 3: Code Generation and Software-in-the-Loop Verification
%[text] In this task you generate embedded C code from the cochlear implant speech processor model with Embedded Coder, then verify that code with Software-in-the-Loop (SIL) simulation. In SIL mode, Simulink generates production code from the model, compiles it for your host computer, and runs it in place of the model. Comparing a normal simulation with a SIL simulation shows that the generated code behaves exactly like the design you verified.
%[text] You will:
%[text] - Configure the model for embedded code generation (`ert.tlc`).
%[text] - Generate code, inspect the generated code, check its code metrics, and trace code back to blocks
%[text] - (Optional) Run the model in SIL mode.
%[text] - (Optional) Verify the SIL results in the Simulation Data Inspector and confirm the requirements are still met. \
%%
%[text] ## Setup
%[text] Store the model name in a variable so every command below uses it.
mdl = "CochlearImplant_SIL";
%[text] Requirement sets loaded in an earlier task share their names with the ones used here, so close them first. This discards unsaved edits to any open requirement set.
slreq.clear
%[text] Open the model. Its `PostLoadFcn` callback loads `speech_dft.mat`, which contains the speech recording `y`, its sample rate `fs`, and the timeseries `in`. The root-level `In1` port reads `in` from the workspace. SIL needs this root-level input because the generated code receives its input data through `In1`.
open_system(mdl)
%[text] ![](text:image:8035)
in
%%
%[text] ### Configure the model for embedded code generation
%[text] Select the Embedded Coder system target file, `ert.tlc`. ERT (Embedded Real-Time) generates efficient, readable C code for production embedded systems.
set_param(mdl, "SystemTargetFile", "ert.tlc")
%[text] SIL compiles the generated code for the computer running MATLAB, so the hardware implementation settings must describe that computer. MATLAB Online runs on 64-bit Linux with either an Intel or an AMD processor. The local function `hostHardwareDevice`, at the end of this script, detects the processor vendor and returns the matching device type.
hwDevice = hostHardwareDevice()
set_param(mdl, "ProdHWDeviceType", hwDevice)
%[text] Generate a code generation report with a static code metrics report and hyperlinks between code and model, so you can measure the code and trace in both directions in section 2. Turn on signal logging so the simulation results are recorded in the Simulation Data Inspector.
set_param(mdl, ...
    "GenerateReport", "on", ...
    "LaunchReport", "off", ...
    "GenerateCodeMetricsReport", "on", ...
    "IncludeHyperlinkInReport", "on", ...
    "GenerateTraceInfo", "on", ...
    "SignalLogging", "off")
warning('off', 'coder_trace:trace:SourceCodeChange');
%[text] To see these settings in the UI, open the Configuration Parameters dialog box (**Modeling** tab \> **Model Settings**) and go to **Code Generation** and **Hardware Implementation**.
%[text] ![](text:image:6643)
%[text] ![](text:image:3c58)
%[text] ![](text:image:0b6f)
%%
%[text] ## 1. Inspect the Generated Code
%[text] ### Generate the production code
%[text] Generate code with `slbuild`. This may take a few minutes.
slbuild(mdl)
%%
%[text] ### Open the code generation report
%[text] Open the report to browse the code.
coder.report.open(mdl)
%[text] ![](text:image:283f)
%[text] You can also view the code next to the model. On the **Apps** tab, open **Embedded Coder**. The **Code** panel shows the same files with the same hyperlinks.
%[text] ![](text:image:962c)
%%
%[text] ### Check the code metrics
%[text] Before you deploy code to a small, low-power processor such as the one in a cochlear implant, check its size, memory use, and complexity. In the report, click **Static Code Metrics Report** in the navigation pane. You can get the same data at the command line:
metrics = coder.api.getCodeMetrics(mdl);
buildDir = RTW.getBuildDir(char(mdl)).BuildDirectory;
%[text] ![](text:image:8c26)
%[text] **Files.** These are the generated files and their lines of code:
files = metrics.FileInformation;
[fileFolders, fileNames, fileExts] = fileparts(string({files.Name})');
generated = fileFolders == buildDir & [files.NumCodeLines]' > 0;
files = files(generated);
fileNames = fileNames(generated);
fileExts = fileExts(generated);
fileMetrics = table(fileNames + fileExts, [files.NumCodeLines]', [files.NumTotalLines]', ...
    VariableNames=["File", "LinesOfCode", "TotalLines"])
%[text] - `CochlearImplant_SIL.c` holds the algorithm. `CochlearImplant_SIL_initialize` sets the initial states, and `CochlearImplant_SIL_step` runs once per sample time.
%[text] - `CochlearImplant_SIL.h` declares the model's data structures. The root input is `CochlearImplant_SIL_U.In1` and the outputs are `CochlearImplant_SIL_Y.Out1` to `Out8`.
%[text] - `CochlearImplant_SIL_data.c` holds constant parameters, such as the filter coefficients.
%[text] - `ert_main.c` is an example main program that shows how an embedded application calls the step function. It is not part of the metrics. \
%[text] **Global variables.** These are the RAM the model needs, in bytes:
globals = metrics.GlobalVariableInformation;
globalMetrics = table(string({globals.Name})', [globals.Size]', ...
    VariableNames=["Variable", "Bytes"])
%[text] - `CochlearImplant_SIL_DW` (DWork) holds the block states: filter delay lines, buffers, and the Integer Delays of the pulse generator. It takes almost all the RAM.
%[text] - `CochlearImplant_SIL_U` is the input: one `double` sample, 8 bytes.
%[text] - `CochlearImplant_SIL_Y` is the output: 8 channels of `double`, 64 bytes.
%[text] - `CochlearImplant_SIL_M_` is the real-time model object, which holds the error status and timing information. \
%[text] **Functions.** For each generated function, the metrics show its lines of code, its cyclomatic complexity (the number of independent paths through it), and its stack use in bytes:
fcns = metrics.FunctionInformation;
fcns = fcns(startsWith(string({fcns.File}), buildDir) & [fcns.NumCodeLines] > 0);
functionMetrics = table(string({fcns.Name})', [fcns.NumCodeLines]', [fcns.Complexity]', [fcns.StackTotal]', ...
    VariableNames=["Function", "LinesOfCode", "Complexity", "StackBytes"])
%[text] All of the signal processing is in `CochlearImplant_SIL_step`, so it has the most code, the highest complexity, and the largest stack. Compare these numbers with the RAM, ROM, and stack budget of your target processor.
%[text] **Try it:** The model uses double precision. How many bytes of RAM do you expect `CochlearImplant_SIL_DW` to need if the model used `single` instead?
%%
%[text] ### Trace code back to the model
%[text] Every block in the code is identified by a tag like `'<S9>/Abs'`, which means block `Abs` inside subsystem `<S9>`. The header file lists which subsystem each tag stands for. Find the tag for the filter bank's Rectifier subsystem:
header = readlines(fullfile(buildDir, mdl + ".h"));
rectLine = header(contains(header, "Filter bank signal processing/Subsystem/Rectifier'"))
rectSys = extractBetween(rectLine, "'", "'");
rectSys = rectSys(1)
%[text] List the lines of code that implement the Rectifier's absolute value blocks. Requirement SP-03 asks for full-wave rectification, so this is where SP-03 is implemented.
source = readlines(fullfile(buildDir, mdl + ".c"));
source(contains(source, "Abs: '" + rectSys + "/Abs"))
%[text] Pass a tag to `hilite_system` to highlight the block it refers to in the model:
hilite_system(rectSys + "/Abs")
%[text] In the report or the Code panel, you get the same result by clicking the hyperlinked tag in a code comment, such as `'<S9>/Abs'`. Simulink opens the subsystem and highlights the block.
%[text] ![](text:image:1606)
%%
%[text] ## (Optional to run in installed MATLAB)
%[text] ## 2. Software-in-the-Loop Verification
%[text] ### Run a normal simulation as the baseline
%[text] Clear old runs from the Simulation Data Inspector, then simulate the model in Normal mode. This run is the reference that the generated code must match.
Simulink.sdi.clear
set_param(mdl, "SimulationMode", "normal", "SignalLogging", "off");
sim(mdl);
normalRun = Simulink.sdi.getCurrentSimulationRun(mdl);
normalRun.Name = "Normal";
%[text] ### Run the generated code with SIL
%[text] Switch the simulation mode to SIL and simulate again. Simulink generates code, compiles it into a host executable, and exchanges data with it at every time step. The first SIL run takes a few minutes because of the build. Later runs reuse the executable until you change the model.
set_param(mdl, "SimulationMode", "Software-in-the-loop (SIL)")
sim(mdl);
silRun = Simulink.sdi.getCurrentSimulationRun(mdl);
silRun.Name = "SIL";
%[text] You can do the same thing in the Simulink toolstrip. On the **Apps** tab, open **SIL/PIL Manager**, set the simulation mode to **Software-in-the-loop (SIL)**, and click **Run**. While the model runs, the Diagnostic Viewer shows the build steps and `### Starting SIL simulation for component: CochlearImplant_SIL`.
%[text] ![](text:image:466a)
%%
%[text] ## (Optional to run in installed MATLAB)
%[text] ## 3. Verification
%[text] ### Compare the normal and SIL runs in the Simulation Data Inspector
%[text] Open the Simulation Data Inspector. The **Normal** and **SIL** runs each contain the eight channels `Ch0` to `Ch7`, twice: once as the root-level outputs `Out1` to `Out8`, and once as the logged outputs of the `Demux` block. They also contain the logged input signal. Plot a channel from each run to see that they overlap.
Simulink.sdi.view
%[text] ![](text:image:9b06)
%[text] Compare the two runs. The model computes in double precision, so allow a tiny absolute tolerance for floating-point rounding differences between the simulation engine and the compiled code.
cmp = Simulink.sdi.compareRuns(normalRun.ID, silRun.ID, AbsTol=1e-12);
results = arrayfun(@(k) cmp.getResultByIndex(k), 1:cmp.Count);
results = results(string([results.Status]) ~= "Unaligned");
blockPaths = arrayfun(@(r) string(Simulink.sdi.getSignal(r.SignalID1).BlockPath), results);
comparison = table(string({results.Name})', blockPaths', string([results.Status])', ...
    [results.MaxDifference]', VariableNames=["Signal", "Block", "Status", "MaxDifference"])
%[text] Every signal that appears in both runs is `WithinTolerance`. The table leaves out signals that only one run contains. The Normal run also logs signals inside subsystems, such as `PulseBus`, but SIL can log only signals that exist at the top level of the generated code, so those signals have no match.
%[text] To do the comparison interactively, open the **Compare** tab in the Simulation Data Inspector, set **Baseline** to **Normal** and **Compare to** to **SIL**, set **Global Abs Tolerance** to `1e-12`, and click **Compare**.
%%
%[text] ## Summary
%[text] - The model generates embedded C code with Embedded Coder (`ert.tlc`), built for the host processor.
%[text] - The static code metrics report the size, RAM, stack, and complexity of the generated code.
%[text] - Hyperlinks between the code and the model trace each line of code to its block, and each block to its code.
%[text] - (Optional) SIL ran the compiled code against the same input as the normal simulation, and the outputs matched within `1e-12`. \
%%
%[text] ## Local Functions
function device = hostHardwareDevice()
% Return the Hardware Implementation device type that matches the 64-bit
% x86 computer running MATLAB, so the SIL executable is built for it.
switch computer("arch")
    case "glnxa64"
        cpuInfo = fileread("/proc/cpuinfo");
        os = "Linux 64";
    case "win64"
        cpuInfo = getenv("PROCESSOR_IDENTIFIER");
        os = "Windows64";
    otherwise
        error("Ex2Task3:UnsupportedHost", ...
            "This exercise runs SIL on a 64-bit x86 Linux or Windows computer, " + ...
            "but MATLAB is running on '%s'.", computer("arch"));
end
if contains(cpuInfo, "AuthenticAMD")
    vendor = "AMD";
else
    vendor = "Intel";
end
device = vendor + "->x86-64 (" + os + ")";
end

